{"title":"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 23","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems (page does not exist)\">1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) #22</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems\">1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) #23</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A cube measures 10 by 10 by 10 cm. Three cuts are made parallel to the faces of the cube as shown (in three perpendicular directions) creating eight separate solids which are then separated. What is the increase in the total surface area?\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p><i>An image is supposed to go here.  You can help us out by creating one and <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit\">editing it in</a></span>.  Thanks.</i>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/b/59bcfe6832a1a3a31014c9f687577f8e248901af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 300 \\text{cm}^2 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 800 \\text{cm}^2 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 1200 \\text{cm}^2 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 600 \\text{cm}^2 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 0 \\text{cm}^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"554\" height=\"19\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The original surface area was <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/8/038cbb1d9e617e72bd08b24229b155356ef03ffc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6(10^2) = 600$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"99\" height=\"19\" /> square centimeters. The surface area of the separate solids are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/8/2f889bd598c6cc0ead706d741c0685e579917e2e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8(6 \\cdot 5^2) = 1200$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"121\" height=\"19\" /> cubic centimeters. This is an increase of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/1/0a1e53fc273532134bdd3062cb67528d15ec8ab1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\text{(D)} \\quad 600}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"81\" height=\"28\" /> square centimeters.\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)\">1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Answer_Key\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Pr